Let \(\mathcal{T}_0:=\mathcal{T}_{x_0}^{	heta_0}\), \(\mathcal{N}_0:=\mathcal{N}_{x_0}^{	heta_0}\), and choose orthonormal matrices \(U_{\mathcal{T}_0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d	imes 	exttt{k}}\) and \(U_{\mathcal{N}_0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d	imes(d-	exttt{k})}\) spanning these spaces. For \((u,v)\) near \((0,0)\), write \(x(u,v):=x_0+U_{\mathcal{T}_0}u+U_{\mathcal{N}_0}v\) and define \[ \Phi(	heta,u,v):=U_{\mathcal{N}_0}^{	op}
abla_x g(	heta,x(u,v)). \] By \Cref{prop:geometry}, \(U_{\mathcal{N}_0}^{	op}
abla^2_{xx}g(	heta_0,x_0)U_{\mathcal{N}_0}\) is positive definite. The implicit function theorem therefore gives a \(\mathcal{C}^{2}\) map \(h(	heta,u)\), defined for \((	heta,u)\) near \((	heta_0,0)\), such that \(\Phi(	heta,u,h(	heta,u))=0\). Set \[ \psi(	heta,u):=x_0+U_{\mathcal{T}_0}u+U_{\mathcal{N}_0}h(	heta,u). \] For each fixed \(	heta\), this parametrizes the \(	exttt{k}\)-dimensional manifold \[ \widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta):=\{x:\ U_{\mathcal{N}_0}^{	op}
abla_x g(	heta,x)=0\} \] near \(x_0\). This last statement is local in the following precise sense. The IFT gives open neighborhoods \(\mathcal U_	heta^{0}\) of \(	heta_0\), \(\mathcal U_u^{0}\) of \(0\in\mathbb{R}^{	exttt{k}}\), and \(\mathcal U_v^{0}\) of \(0\in\mathbb{R}^{d-	exttt{k}}\) such that every solution of \(U_{\mathcal{N}_0}^{	op}
abla_x g(	heta,x_0+U_{\mathcal{T}_0}u+U_{\mathcal{N}_0}v)=0\) with \((	heta,u,v)\in\mathcal U_	heta^{0}	imes\mathcal U_u^{0}	imes\mathcal U_v^{0}\) is uniquely represented as \(v=h(	heta,u)\). We now choose a connected open set \(\mathcal U_u\) containing \(0\) whose closure is contained in \(\mathcal U_u^{0}\), shrink the parameter neighborhood to \(\mathcal U_	heta\subseteq\mathcal U_	heta^{0}\), and choose an ambient neighborhood \(\mathcal W\) of \(x_0\) small enough so that every \(x\in\mathcal W\) can be written uniquely as \(x=x_0+U_{\mathcal{T}_0}u+U_{\mathcal{N}_0}v\) with \(u\in\mathcal U_u^{0}\) and \(v\in\mathcal U_v^{0}\), and such that the points of the graph over \(\mathcal U_u\) lie in \(\mathcal W\). With these choices, the local piece of \(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\) inside \(\mathcal W\) is exactly the graph: for every \(	heta\in\mathcal U_	heta\), \[ \widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W = \{\psi(	heta,u):u\in\mathcal U_u\}. \] Finally, since every point of \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\) is a lower-level minimizer and hence satisfies the full first-order condition \(
abla_x g(	heta,x)=0\), it satisfies the projected condition defining \(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\) and therefore \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W\subseteq\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W\). It remains to show that this inclusion is equality on a smaller neighborhood. By the fixed-dimension conclusion in \Cref{prop:geometry} and the local Hausdorff continuity of the solution manifolds \citep[Lem.~3.2]{masiha2025superquantile}, after shrinking \(\mathcal U_	heta\) and \(\mathcal W\), for every \(	heta\in\mathcal U_	heta\), \[ \dim\mathcal{S}(	heta)=	exttt{k} \qquad	ext{and}\qquad \mathcal{S}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W
eq\emptyset . \] For such a \(	heta\), define \[ Z_	heta:=\{u\in\mathcal U_u:\psi(	heta,u)\in\mathcal{S}(	heta)\}. \] The set \(Z_	heta\) records which points of the graph for \(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\) are genuine lower-level minimizers. It is nonempty by the local Hausdorff-continuity step above: since \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W
eq\emptyset\) and \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W\subseteq\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W\), at least one point of \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W\) has the form \(\psi(	heta,u)\). It is closed relative to \(\mathcal U_u\) because \[ Z_	heta=\{u\in\mathcal U_u:\psi(	heta,u)\in\mathcal{S}(	heta)\} = \psi_	heta^{-1}(\mathcal{S}(	heta))\cap\mathcal U_u, \qquad \psi_	heta(u):=\psi(	heta,u), \] where \(\psi_	heta\) is continuous and \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\) is compact, hence closed, by \Cref{prop:geometry}. We next show that \(Z_	heta\) is open. Take \(u_1\in Z_	heta\) and set \(x_1:=\psi(	heta,u_1)\). Near \(x_1\), both \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\) and \(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\) are embedded \(	exttt{k}\)-dimensional submanifolds, and we already know that \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\subseteq\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\). Consider the inclusion map \[ i:\mathcal{S}(	heta)\hookrightarrow\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta),\qquad i(x)=x. \] This map is smooth because both manifolds are embedded submanifolds of the same ambient space. Its derivative at \(x_1\) is the identity map on tangent vectors: \[ D i(x_1):\mathcal{T}_{x_1}\mathcal{S}(	heta)	o \mathcal{T}_{x_1}\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta),\qquad v\mapsto v. \] Since the two tangent spaces both have dimension \(	exttt{k}\), this derivative is an isomorphism. The inverse function theorem for manifolds therefore gives a neighborhood \(\mathcal O_S\) of \(x_1\) in \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\) such that \(i(\mathcal O_S)\) is an open neighborhood of \(x_1\) in \(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\). But \(i\) is just inclusion, so \(i(\mathcal O_S)=\mathcal O_S\subseteq\mathcal{S}(	heta)\). Thus some neighborhood of \(x_1\) inside \(\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\) is contained in \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\). Pulling this neighborhood back through the chart \(u\mapsto\psi(	heta,u)\), we obtain a neighborhood of \(u_1\) contained in \(Z_	heta\). Since this argument works for every \(u_1\in Z_	heta\), each point of \(Z_	heta\) has a relative neighborhood in \(\mathcal U_u\) contained in \(Z_	heta\). Thus \(Z_	heta\) is open in \(\mathcal U_u\). Now \(Z_	heta\) is nonempty and is both closed and open relative to \(\mathcal U_u\). If \(Z_	heta
eq\mathcal U_u\), then \(Z_	heta\) and \(\mathcal U_u\setminus Z_	heta\) would form a separation of \(\mathcal U_u\): two nonempty disjoint relatively open sets whose union is \(\mathcal U_u\). This contradicts the connectedness of \(\mathcal U_u\). Hence \(Z_	heta=\mathcal U_u\). Therefore every point of the local graph \(\{\psi(	heta,u):u\in\mathcal U_u\}\) belongs to \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\). Together with the earlier inclusion \(\mathcal{S}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W\subseteq\widetilde{\mathcal{S}}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W\), this gives \[ \mathcal{S}(	heta)\cap\mathcal W = \{\psi(	heta,u):u\in\mathcal U_u\}, \] after shrinking \(\mathcal W\) within the chart neighborhood if needed.
